## SLIDE 11: SERVICE MESH & SECURITY
**Duration: 1 minute 30 seconds**

### Key Points:
- **Istio Service Mesh**: Intelligent traffic management and security
- **mTLS**: Mutual TLS encryption for all service-to-service communication
- **JWT Validation**: API authentication with JWT tokens
- **RBAC**: Role-based access control (Admin, Moderator, User, Guest)
- **Network Policies**: Default deny, explicit allow rules
- **Audit Logging**: All access logged for compliance

### Speaker Notes:
Security is built into Laniakea from day one.

**Service Mesh**: Istio sits between all services and handles communication. It automatically encrypts traffic with mutual TLS (mTLS). This means every service-to-service communication is encrypted. Even if an attacker gains access to your network, they can't read the traffic.

**JWT Validation**: When a user logs in, they receive a JWT token. This token is validated by the API Gateway. Only valid tokens are allowed to make API calls.

**RBAC**: Laniakea supports role-based access control. Admins have full access. Moderators can manage content. Users can access their own resources. Guests have read-only access.

**Network Policies**: By default, all traffic is denied. You explicitly allow communication between services. This prevents lateral movement if an attacker compromises a service.

**Audit Logging**: Every access is logged. You can see who accessed what, when, and from where. This is critical for compliance.

---

## SLIDE 12: OBSERVABILITY STACK
**Duration: 1 minute 30 seconds**

### Key Points:
- **Metrics (Prometheus)**: CPU, memory, latency, error rate, throughput
- **Visualization (Grafana)**: Dashboards showing real-time metrics
- **Tracing (Jaeger)**: Distributed tracing across services
- **Logging (ELK)**: Centralized log aggregation and search
- **Alerting**: Automatic alerts on critical metrics
- **SLOs**: Service Level Objectives for reliability tracking

### Speaker Notes:
You can't manage what you can't measure. Laniakea includes a comprehensive observability stack.

**Prometheus** collects metrics from all services. CPU usage, memory usage, request latency, error rate, throughput. Metrics are collected every 15 seconds.

**Grafana** visualizes these metrics in real-time dashboards. You can see at a glance how your metaverse is performing. Is latency increasing? Are error rates spiking? You see it immediately.

**Jaeger** provides distributed tracing. When a user makes a request, Jaeger traces it across all services. You can see exactly where time is being spent. Is the bottleneck in the WebXR Gateway? In Spatial Sync? In the database?

**ELK Stack** aggregates logs from all services. You can search logs, filter by service, by error level, by user. If something goes wrong, you can quickly find the root cause.

**Alerting** is automatic. If latency exceeds a threshold, you get an alert. If error rate spikes, you get an alert. Your operations team can respond immediately.

**SLOs** (Service Level Objectives) track reliability. You might set an SLO of 99.9% availability. Laniakea tracks whether you're meeting that SLO.

---

## SLIDE 13: SCALING & PERFORMANCE
**Duration: 1 minute 30 seconds**

### Key Points:
- **Horizontal Pod Autoscaling**: Auto-scales based on CPU, memory, custom metrics
- **Cluster Autoscaling**: Adds/removes nodes based on demand
- **Performance Targets**: < 100ms end-to-end latency, 10,000 entities per cluster
- **Spatial Partitioning**: Divides world into cells for efficient updates
- **Message Batching**: Reduces network overhead
- **Compression**: Reduces bandwidth usage

### Speaker Notes:
Laniakea is designed to scale to millions of concurrent users.

**Horizontal Pod Autoscaling**: When CPU usage exceeds 70%, Kubernetes automatically adds more replicas of a service. When CPU drops, it removes replicas. This ensures you're always using the right amount of resources.

**Cluster Autoscaling**: When pods are pending (waiting for resources), Kubernetes adds more nodes to the cluster. When nodes are underutilized, it removes them. This optimizes cost.

**Performance Optimization**: We use several techniques to ensure low latency:
- **Spatial Partitioning**: The world is divided into cells. When updating entity positions, we only send updates to nearby cells. This reduces network overhead.
- **Message Batching**: Instead of sending individual updates, we batch them. This reduces the number of network packets.
- **Compression**: Messages are compressed with Zstandard. This reduces bandwidth usage.

With these optimizations, we maintain < 100ms end-to-end latency even with 10,000 concurrent entities.

